Why this is not the same licence as the artifact
------------------------------------------------

Because Creative Commons says not to use theirs on software, and they are right.

From the Creative Commons FAQ, at creativecommons.org/faq/, under "Can I apply a
Creative Commons license to software?":

    We recommend against using Creative Commons licenses for software.

and, earlier on the same page:

    The only categories of works for which CC does not recommend its licenses
    are computer software and hardware.

Their reasons are the ones that matter here. A CC licence has no notion of source
code as distinct from the thing built from it, says nothing about patents, and
says nothing about linking — three questions that do not arise for a drawing and
do arise for a program. Applying CC BY-NC-ND to `tools/` would be putting an
instrument to a use its own author warns against, and getting a document whose
terms do not address the situation it is in.

All rights reserved is not a stricter version of the same idea dressed up. It is a
different and simpler statement: nothing is granted, so nothing has to be
construed. For code that exists to produce one specific artifact for one specific
profile, that is the accurate description of the position, and it does not pretend
to be a licence when it is the absence of one.

The practical difference between the two files: the artifact may be shared
verbatim, for non-commercial purposes, with attribution. The generator may not be
shared at all. That asymmetry is deliberate. The banner is meant to be seen and
pointed at. The machine that writes it is not meant to be run by anyone else,
because run by anyone else it writes a different banner from a different history,
which is the whole of what `tools/attest.ts` exists to say.


